  "content": {
    "id": "p22-q5-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "5",
    "prompt": "Earlier we mentioned that lines with equal gradient are parallel. That is, they never intersect (provided they are distinct lines). Let’s see what happens when we try to find their point of intersection.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p22-q5-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Attempt to find the point of intersection of the lines whose equations are $y=x+1$ and $y=x+2$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "No point of intersection: equating the expressions for $y$ gives the contradiction $1=2$.",
          "worked": "At a point of intersection, both expressions for $y$ must be equal.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}x+1&=x+2\\\\1&=2\\end{align*}$$\n\nThis is a contradiction, so the lines have no point of intersection.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p22-q5-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Consider the lines with equations $y=mx+c_1$ and $y=mx+c_2$ where $c_1\\ne c_2$. Prove that these lines have no point of intersection.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "An intersection would require $c_1=c_2$, contradicting $c_1\\ne c_2$. Hence there is no point of intersection.",
          "worked": "Suppose the lines intersect. Their expressions for $y$ must then be equal.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}mx+c_1&=mx+c_2\\\\c_1&=c_2\\end{align*}$$\n\nThis contradicts $c_1\\ne c_2$. Therefore the lines have no point of intersection.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      }
    ]
  },
